diff --git a/docs/shiploop/architecture.md b/docs/shiploop/architecture.md
index 3fba9bb..7b38ab8 100644
--- a/docs/shiploop/architecture.md
+++ b/docs/shiploop/architecture.md
@@ -47,15 +47,21 @@ As of cc92991f2e24. This file records what exists and proposes no change.
 - AD-2 (inherited) Source and test files are CommonJS modules in strict mode, each ending with one module.exports object that names its public functions, and built-ins are required with the node: prefix. Why: the package declares the module type and states no reason (inferred). Evidence: `package.json` `type`; `src/users.js` `module.exports`.
 - AD-3 (inherited) HTTP routing is hand-written inside the one request listener that createServer builds: it turns the request target into a URL, branches on method and path, with an exact comparison for the list and an anchored pattern for one user, and ends in the not-found answer. Each route is a branch in that listener. Why: the README says the server is built on the built-in HTTP module (stated: `README.md:8`). Evidence: `src/server.js` `createServer`.
 - AD-4 (inherited) Every answer goes through one helper, send, which sets only the JSON content type and writes the compact JSON of the body; an error answer is an object with a single error property holding a constant text. Why: one response shape serves every route (inferred). Evidence: `src/server.js` `send`.
-- AD-5 (inherited) Failures reach HTTP callers only as that error answer, and only for the one case the code anticipates, not found with status 404. Helper functions instead signal bad arguments by throwing a type error or a range error, and the listener has no try or catch, so a thrown error is not turned into a JSON answer. Why: the repository documents no error policy (inferred). Evidence: `src/paginate.js` `paginate`; `src/strings.js` `truncate`; `src/server.js` `createServer`.
 - AD-6 (inherited) State is a module-level constant array of user records in the users module; callers read it only through getUser, which returns the stored record, and listUsers, which returns a new list of the same records; the server never touches the array and no function writes it. Why: the README calls it an in-memory user store (stated: `README.md:7`). Evidence: `src/users.js` `USERS`.
 - AD-7 (inherited) The one configuration value, the listening port, is read from the PORT environment variable once, inside the block that runs only when the server file is the program entry, with a fallback of 3000; modules read no environment when loaded and createServer takes no options. Why: loading the server for tests must not bind a port (inferred). Evidence: `src/server.js` `require.main`.
 - AD-8 (inherited) Building a server and listening are separate steps: createServer returns a server that is not listening, and the caller picks the port and address. Tests start it on an ephemeral port on 127.0.0.1, call it over real HTTP with the global fetch and close it in a finally block; nothing is mocked. Why: the code comment says the server is not listening until the caller calls listen (stated: `src/server.js` `createServer`). Evidence: `test/server.test.js` `withServer`.
 - AD-9 (inherited) Tests use the runtime's built-in runner with strict assertions; each source module has a matching test file named after it; the test command is the runner's own file discovery with no configuration; test names are sentences that state the behaviour. Why: the README gives the test command as the runner (stated: `README.md:10`). Evidence: `package.json` `scripts`; `test/users.test.js` "listUsers returns every user".
 - AD-10 (inherited) Requests are answered synchronously from memory in the listener that receives them; there are no timers, queues, workers or shutdown hooks, so no background work exists to coordinate with. Why: nothing in the repository starts such work (inferred). Evidence: `src/server.js` `createServer`.
 - AD-11 (inherited) There is no identity or access layer: the listener looks only at the method and the path, treats every caller alike and returns stored user records whole, email included. Why: no document states an access policy and the data are fixed example records (inferred). Evidence: `src/server.js` `createServer`; `src/users.js` `USERS`.
-- AD-12 (inherited) Helper modules are pure, argument-checking functions that stand alone: the paging helper and the text helpers are not used by the server today and only their tests call them, and the paging helper reports the page, the page count and the items but not the list length or the size. Why: the README lists them as separate modules beside the server and states no use for them (inferred). Evidence: `src/paginate.js` `paginate`; `src/strings.js` `capitalize`; `test/paginate.test.js` "paginate returns the requested page of an evenly divided list".
+- AD-13 (accepted) Replaces AD-12. The server pages the listing with the existing paging helper, and the helper's page count is the list length divided by the size, rounded up, so a trailing partial page counts. The helper's result stays exactly page, pages and items, as a strict-equality test pins it, so the server adds size and total to its own answer. The text helpers stay standalone and unused by the server. Why: S4 puts the first size matches on page 1 and S5 defines pages as the pages the matches fill, but the helper rounded down, which would answer pages 0 and no items for the three stored users at the default size 10 (the conflict recorded in R-5); one rounding expression fixes it, the existing tests (an even split, size 0) are unaffected, and the repository keeps one paging implementation. Alternatives: page inside the server and leave the helper (rebuilds a capability and leaves its defect); add size and total to the helper's result (fails the existing strict-equality test, S8); put the size limit in the helper (R-5 records no upper limit and no sentence asks for one). Basis: S4, S5. Evidence: `src/paginate.js` `paginate`; `test/paginate.test.js` "paginate returns the requested page of an evenly divided list".
+- AD-14 (accepted) Replaces AD-5. A page or size that is not valid is a second anticipated failure, besides not found: HTTP 400 through send, with an error property holding constant text, one sentence per bad parameter, joined when both are bad, and never echoing the value. Helper functions still signal bad arguments by throwing and the listener still has no try or catch, because the listener checks page and size first and hands the helper only a finite whole number, so no caller input can make a helper throw (a page text too large for an exact number is held at Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER). Why: S6 asks for 400 with an error that says what is wrong, and an unhandled throw would stop the whole process (R-3, inferred), so the check comes before the helper is called. Alternatives: let the helper's range error surface (stops the process); wrap the listener in try and catch with a 500 answer (changes AD-5 beyond what S6 asks); one fixed sentence for any bad value (does not say what is wrong). Basis: S6. Evidence: `src/server.js` `send`; `src/server.js` `createServer`; `src/paginate.js` `paginate`.
+- AD-15 (accepted) The name filter runs in the /users branch of the listener over the list listUsers returns: the user's name and the query text are lower-cased and compared with includes, never as a regular expression; an absent or empty name keeps every user and id order is kept. Why: S3 asks for plain text, ignoring case; a pattern built from caller text would read . and * as pattern characters and throw on an opening parenthesis, which stops the process because nothing catches; filtering in the listener leaves the store's two read functions as AD-6 names them. Alternatives: a new read function in the users module (the cleaner home, kept out because no sentence asks to change the store); a regular expression with escaped text (heavier than includes); locale-aware comparison (not asked). Basis: S3. Evidence: `src/users.js` `listUsers`; `src/server.js` `createServer`.
+- AD-16 (accepted) The query is read from the URL object the listener already builds, only inside the /users branch. Page and size count as given when present, even if empty. A whole number is decimal digits only, tested with an anchored pattern like the id route's; page must be at least 1 and size from 1 to 100. The defaults (page 1, size 10) and the limit 100 are constants in the server module and not configuration, and the parsing helper is private to that file. A repeated parameter uses its first value and no other parameter is read. Why: S5 and S6 make an empty value given and invalid; a truthiness test would skip it, and a plain number conversion or parse accepts 1e1, 0x10 and a padded 1 and reads 1.5 as 1, which S6 rejects; the id pattern is the repository's own whole-number precedent. Alternatives: converting with Number and testing for an integer (accepts those forms); a shared query-parsing module (one caller); limits read from the environment (nothing asks, AD-7). Basis: S4, S5, S6, S7. Evidence: `src/server.js` `createServer`.
+- AD-17 (accepted) GET /users answers a JSON array (every user, or the users the name matches) unless page or size is given; then it answers 200 with an object holding page, size, total, pages and items. Page and size are the values used, defaults included; total is the matched count before paging; pages and items come from the paging helper; a page after the last answers empty items, also when nothing matched (pages 0). Paging is stateless: each request recomputes its page, with no cursor, session or cache. Why: S5 and S7 split the two shapes on whether page or size is given; a plain request and response read from memory is the lightest channel that meets the stated behaviour (AD-10). Alternatives: always the object (breaks S7); 404 or 400 beyond the last page (contradicts S5); cursors or Link headers (not asked). Basis: S5, S7. Evidence: `src/server.js` `createServer`.
 
 ## Superseded
 
-None. The repository keeps no decision records, so no AD above links one and none is superseded.
+- AD-5 (inherited) Failures reach HTTP callers only as that error answer, and only for the one case the code anticipates, not found with status 404. Helper functions instead signal bad arguments by throwing a type error or a range error, and the listener has no try or catch, so a thrown error is not turned into a JSON answer. Why: the repository documents no error policy (inferred). Evidence: `src/paginate.js` `paginate`; `src/strings.js` `truncate`; `src/server.js` `createServer`. Superseded by AD-14. Basis: S6.
+- AD-12 (inherited) Helper modules are pure, argument-checking functions that stand alone: the paging helper and the text helpers are not used by the server today and only their tests call them, and the paging helper reports the page, the page count and the items but not the list length or the size. Why: the README lists them as separate modules beside the server and states no use for them (inferred). Evidence: `src/paginate.js` `paginate`; `src/strings.js` `capitalize`; `test/paginate.test.js` "paginate returns the requested page of an evenly divided list". Superseded by AD-13. Basis: S4, S5.
+
+The repository keeps no decision records, so no AD links one.
